# Unattended operation

You may be running with no human watching. Behave accordingly.

- Never stop to ask a question you could answer by reading the code. If a
  decision is genuinely ambiguous, pick the option most consistent with existing
  code, state the assumption in your final report, and continue.
- `.cursorrules` was written for an interactive chat session. When it conflicts
  with running unattended, this rule wins. Specifically: do not offer "2
  implementations + tradeoff" and wait — pick one and implement it; do not
  truncate your final report to "code + 3-line docstring" — the report format
  below is required. Every other mandate in `.cursorrules` (TDD, strict TS, no
  `any`, `asyncHandler`, no `console.log`, fixed ports) still applies in full.
- Read `AGENTS.md` first. Its Commands table is what actually works in a fresh
  clone; its Never list is enforced by `.cursor/hooks/guard-shell.mjs`.
- Verify with commands, not by reading. "The test should pass" is not evidence;
  the test output is. Build `@scholaracle/contracts` before type-checking.
- Prefer the smallest diff that fully satisfies the task. Resist improving
  adjacent code. Do not reformat files you did not otherwise change.
- A green PR here auto-merges and deploys to production, unless it touches the
  gated paths listed in `AGENTS.md` ("The gate": workflows, EAS/native/app
  config, dependencies, Dockerfiles, Railway config, the guardrails). Those PRs
  wait for a human; never route around that. If you are not confident the
  change is safe to ship, prefix the PR title with `[needs-human]` and say why
  in the body.
- Before declaring done, run `git status`. Uncommitted work and stray files mean
  you are not done.
- Your final message will be read by a program and a tired human. Lead with the
  outcome, then what changed, then what you verified (commands and results),
  then what you deliberately did not do.
